---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Effect Studio — Varve Docs"
  description="Use Effect Studio to preview, tune, apply, cancel, and manage named editable Object Filter treatments in Varve."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <article class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Tools / Effect Studio
      </nav>

      <h1>Effect Studio</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Effect Studio is the creative discovery surface for object-local effects. It previews a
        candidate against the accepted document state, then commits the named recipe through the
        normal document transaction and history systems.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Browse, adjust, preview</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Select one compatible object, or select several objects when you want to apply the same treatment to all of them.</li>
          <li>Open <strong>Effect Studio</strong>, search the Treatment Gallery, and choose <strong>Adjust</strong> to expose the recipe controls.</li>
          <li>Choose <strong>Preview</strong>. The comparison renders the accepted state as Before and the current candidate as After.</li>
          <li>Use the split slider or Before, After, and Compare modes. Fit, 100%, and 200% change only the preview viewport; drag a zoomed image to inspect its edges and choose Center to restore the origin.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          With a multi-selection, the comparison shows the first selected object as a representative
          preview. Applying still affects every selected object; direct tuning is available after
          selecting one object.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Fit and inspection scale</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Fit</strong> contains the complete encoded editing preview in the stage. <strong>100%</strong>
          is a true 1:1 view of that preview bitmap—one CSS pixel per encoded preview pixel—and
          <strong>200%</strong> doubles that scale. These controls never reposition the document or
          change the main canvas camera. At 100% and 200%, drag the preview to pan both comparison
          sides together, or choose <strong>Center</strong> to return to the middle.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Effect Studio bitmap is a bounded editing preview, not a full-resolution export. Use
          the canvas or an exported proof when every source pixel matters. Shared image paints and
          shapeless image geometry are resolved before the preview frame is fitted, so a current
          wide image is not cropped to an older node size.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Accepted state versus current candidate</h2>
        <p>
          Before is the accepted document captured when the draft or applied-treatment edit began.
          After is the current candidate, including only the preview-owned or live-edited changes.
          The comparison never disables the whole Object Filter stack to manufacture an original
          image, because that would also hide unrelated accepted effects.
        </p>
        <dl class="state-list">
          <div><dt>Draft preview</dt><dd>Preview changes are provisional until Keep treatment or Add to stack is chosen.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Live tuning</dt><dd>Editing an applied recipe updates that recipe in place while the accepted baseline remains available for comparison.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Cancel</dt><dd>Removes only filters owned by the active preview session and keeps unrelated edits made during the session.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Apply</dt><dd>Creates one normal undoable document edit. Reapplying an existing treatment requires the explicit Add another action.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Read the preview status</h2>
        <p>
          A preview image carries its source, variant, revision identity, renderer, quality tier,
          warnings, and fallback state. A provisional result means a bounded render completed while
          a font or raster resource was still settling; the UI discloses that state and retries the
          latest matching request. Empty, canceled, missing, and failed results remain distinct from
          a successful authoritative frame.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Where advanced editing lives</h2>
        <p>
          Effect Studio owns named treatment intent. Open <strong>Appearance → Object Filters</strong>
          when you need per-effect visibility, order, opacity, blend mode, or a raw parameter.
          Editing a derived member preserves the named treatment and marks it Customized. Use
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/image-treatments')}>Image Treatments</a> for photo-local
          correction and Adjustment Layers for effects that intentionally cross object boundaries.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Export and persistence</h2>
        <p>
          Applying a treatment stores its editable Object Filter recipe and provenance, not a
          preview bitmap. The canvas and export compositor consume the document stack. If an export
          target cannot represent a local effect, follow the export report and verify the smallest
          affected region rather than assuming the document was flattened.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Related guides</h2>
        <p><a href={sitePath('/features/effect-studio')}>Effect Studio feature overview</a> · <a href={sitePath('/features/layer-effects')}>Layer Effects</a> · <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/color')}>Color and Effects</a></p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .breadcrumb { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: .875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a, .docs-section a, .docs-cta a { color: var(--text-link); }
  h1 { color: var(--text-primary); font: 700 clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.8rem)/1.1 var(--font-editorial); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .docs-intro { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .docs-section { margin-bottom: 2.75rem; }
  .docs-section h2 { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-accent); color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 1.45rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; padding-bottom: .5rem; }
  .docs-section p, .docs-section li, .state-list dd { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8; }
  .docs-section li { margin-bottom: .5rem; }
  .docs-section strong, .state-list dt { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .state-list { display: grid; gap: .85rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 0; }
  .state-list div { border-left: 2px solid var(--border-accent); padding-left: 1rem; }
  .state-list dt { font-weight: 700; }
  .state-list dd { margin: .25rem 0 0; }
  .docs-cta { border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default); padding-top: 2.5rem; text-align: center; }
  .docs-cta h2 { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .docs-cta p { color: var(--text-secondary); }
</style>
